Post-dump corrections, learning(thru Gemini)
- TTL is used to prevent loop.: If two routers accidentally get misconfigured into a loop, packets would circle each other forever, crashing the network. TTL forces packets to self-destruct (expire) when the counter hits 0.
- SYN is not a protocol: it is a flag inside the TCP packet header. in a SYN Scan, a TCP packet with a SYN flag set is sent. if the port is open response is SYN/ACK and instead of sending back ACK, the attacker sends RST(reset) to prevent getting logged(still leaves a footprint in network packet captures however)
  if closed, the target responds with RST
- Protocols are agreed-upon communication rules: they ensure that a Linux server, an iPhone, and a Windows PC all agree on how to break up, address, encrypt, and reassemble data.
- TCP: connection-oriented
  slower, high reliability
  used for Web(Http/Https) or File Transfer(SSH, FTP)
- UDP: Connectionless
  Quick low reliability(no tracking)
  Video Streaming, Gaming, DNS Queries
- ICMP: Diagnostic, Control
  Ping, Traceroute, error reporting
- A type of protocol for communication is set, chosen by the software program on the device when it asks OS to send info
  When a developer writes a program, they create a Socket(an endpoint for sending/receiving data. Active connection between two machines: source ip, source port, destination ip, destination port) and declare what kind of traffic it will be 
  Web Browsers (Chrome, Firefox): Need 100 percent accuracy (you can't have missing code on a webpage), so they program their sockets to use TCP (Port 80 or 443).
  Online Multiplayer Games / Video Calls (Discord, Zoom): Prioritize live speed over absolute accuracy (a dropped frame is better than freezing the whole stream), so they program their sockets to use UDP.
  Network Diagnostic Tools (ping): Need low-level network status, so the command tells the OS kernel to build an ICMP packet directly.
  Data is created -> Protocol Formatting(ex. OS first executes the three way handshake(TCP) to open a clear connection with the server -> once connected OS takes the data and wraps it inside a TCP header(Source, desstination ports)
  -> among that packaging with other sorts from different layers(IP, MAC, Data suitability), then the Network Interface Card(NIC) in hardware convert the whole package into electrical signals/light pulses and push it onto the physical cable --> routers . .
- Firewalls and tools like Wireshark or Snort are able to inspect these incoming packets in real time because these info are baked right in the packets throughout the encapsulation process, allowing them to find suspicious behaviors through inspection or rules before they reach processing of the servers
- Server: A server is simply a specialized computer that runs $24/7$, connected to the internet, waiting to serve resources, data, or services to other computers (called clients).
- Wifi, cellular data, and hotspots replace the physical ethernet cable(a wired network cord used to connect devices like computers, routers for fast and stable data transfer) with invisible radio waves(layer 1)
  Wifi: High-frequency radio signals operating over short distances (usually within a home, building, or coffee shop). Our machine's internal antenna converts digital pulses(1/0) into radio frequencies. The Wi-Fi router catches those radio waves, converts them back into electrical signals, and pushes them down a physical wire into the broader internet.
  Cellular Data: Long-range radio signals managed by telecom providers (AT&T, Verizon, T-Mobile). Instead of connecting to a wifi router close, your phone transmits radio waves to a massive cell tower miles away. That cell tower is connected to high-speed fiber cables underground, routing your data directly into the carrier’s core network and out to the internet.
  Mobile Hotspot: Turns a mobile phone into a mini wifi router. Your laptop connects to your phone via Wi-Fi radio waves. Your phone takes that data, translates it, and sends it out to the nearest cell tower using its cellular radio antenna. The phone works as a bridge between the laptop and the cellular tower -> internet
- Fast Internet: A combination of high bandwidth (carrying lots of data at once) and low latency (delivering packets with almost zero delay).

